Test: `evaluate([": foo 10 ;", ": foo foo 1 + ;", "foo"])` → `[11]`.

So `foo` must execute the body `foo 1 +` with the new definition of `foo` (the one that pushes 10). If we expand recursively at call time: foo → [foo, 1, +] → inner foo → [foo, 1, +] → ... infinite. So naive recursive expansion fails.

How does real Forth handle this? In real Forth, definitions compile to code that references the current definition of a word at compile time (in immediate/delayed compilation). `: foo foo 1 + ;` — when compiling the body, the inner `foo` is compiled as a reference to the old definition (the one that pushes 10), because the new definition isn't active during its own compilation. So calling foo executes: push 10 (old foo), push 1, add → 11.

So the correct approach is: when defining a word, resolve the body tokens against the current definitions, and store the resolved body (with nested words replaced by their bodies at definition time). So at definition time, expand each token in the body: if a token is a user-defined word, replace it with its (already resolved) body. Numbers and builtins stay as-is.

Let me verify with the tests:

1. `: dup-twice dup dup ;` → dup is a builtin, stays as-is. Body = [dup, dup]. `1 dup-twice` → 1 dup dup → [1,1,1]. ✓

2. `: countup 1 2 3 ;` → [1,2,3]. ✓

3. `: foo dup ;` then `: foo dup dup ;` → the second definition replaces the first. Body [dup, dup]. `1 foo` → [1,1,1]. ✓

4. `: swap dup ;` → body [dup]. `1 swap` → [1,1]. ✓ (overrides builtin)

5. `: + * ;` → body [*]. `3 4 +` → 3 4 * → [12]. ✓

6. `: foo 5 ;`, `: bar foo ;`, `: foo 6 ;`, `bar foo` → bar's body is resolved at definition time: foo → [5]. So bar = [5]. Then foo redefined to [6]. `bar foo` → bar pushes 5, foo pushes 6 → [5, 6]. ✓

7. `: foo 10 ;`, `: foo foo 1 + ;`, `foo` → first foo = [10]. Second definition: body tokens [foo, 1, +], foo resolves to [10] → body = [10, 1, +]. `foo` → push 10, push 1, + → [11]. ✓ No recursion!

8. `: SWAP DUP Dup dup ;` → body [DUP, Dup, dup] — none are user-defined at definition time, so stay as-is. `1 swap` → swap is user-defined → body [DUP, Dup, dup] → each resolves to dup builtin → 1 dup dup dup → [1,1,1,1]. ✓

Great, so the fix is: resolve the body at definition time.

